  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "ZSbJuzmnEwPIj0Xz8ZywtvhooDU=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:45.791Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "twoday was the Danish top pick for partnership and is now filed do-not-pitch. The positive finding survives the demotion: it is a proven multi-vendor carrier, top-tier across Microsoft with Databricks, Snowflake and AWS alongside \u2014 evidence it can take third-party technology to its clients at scale, which is the behaviour a channel partner has to demonstrate.\n\nTwo things overturn it. Being Microsoft-centric means the reference architecture it sells has a strong default gravity toward one vendor's platform, and a client-owned, local-first record layer sits awkwardly against that default. And at 2,200-3,000 people it is too large for a first design partner, which needs a firm where one delivery lead can commit without an alliance review.\n\nThe distinction worth carrying is between carrier capability and first-partner suitability. twoday demonstrably has the former, which is why it ranked first in Denmark before the criteria tightened; the current screen asks for the latter, and the two are not the same test. If a second-wave partner programme ever follows the design-partner phase, proven multi-vendor carriers at this scale are the natural cohort to revisit.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- twoday (DK \u00b7 2,200-3,000)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> WAS DK top pick for partnership. Proven multi-vendor carrier, but Microsoft-centric and too large for a first design partner.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:45.791458Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:45.791463Z"
    }
  },
